1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper studies a puzzling training instability
in behavior cloning.</font>

2
00:00:05,055 --> 00:00:09,787
<font color="#FFFFFF">When you train a policy network with minibatch
stochastic gradient descent,</font>

3
00:00:09,867 --> 00:00:14,278
<font color="#FFFFFF">the long-horizon reward can swing wildly from one
iterate to the next,</font>

4
00:00:14,358 --> 00:00:17,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">even though the behavior cloning loss barely moves.</font>

5
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:22,233
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors trace this to what they call gradient
variance amplification,</font>

6
00:00:22,313 --> 00:00:27,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">where tiny stochastic gradient noise gets amplified
into catastrophic error accumulation</font>

7
00:00:27,959 --> 00:00:31,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">through the closed loop between the policy and its
environment.</font>

8
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:33,974
<font color="#FFFFFF">Most standard fixes do not help,</font>

9
00:00:34,054 --> 00:00:39,941
<font color="#FFFFFF">but taking an exponential moving average of the
weights turns out to be remarkably effective,</font>

10
00:00:40,021 --> 00:00:43,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the same story holds for autoregressive
language models.</font>

11
00:00:44,170 --> 00:00:47,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">Imagine training a robot controller by imitation.</font>

12
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:51,999
<font color="#FFFFFF">You watch the training loss, and it drops smoothly
and stays low.</font>

13
00:00:52,079 --> 00:00:53,526
<font color="#FFFFFF">Everything looks fine.</font>

14
00:00:53,606 --> 00:00:58,244
<font color="#FFFFFF">But if you actually deploy the policy and measure
how well it walks,</font>

15
00:00:58,324 --> 00:01:02,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">the score jumps around violently from one training
step to the next.</font>

16
00:01:03,041 --> 00:01:04,973
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper is about that gap.</font>

17
00:01:05,053 --> 00:01:08,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">The single-step imitation loss is calm and stable,</font>

18
00:01:08,522 --> 00:01:14,201
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet the thing you truly care about, the
long-horizon reward, is oscillating wildly.</font>

19
00:01:14,281 --> 00:01:19,266
<font color="#FFFFFF">And because it is expensive to roll out and
evaluate at every checkpoint,</font>

20
00:01:19,346 --> 00:01:21,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">you usually never see these swings,</font>

21
00:01:21,774 --> 00:01:26,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">which means whichever checkpoint you happen to grab
could be a bad one.</font>

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,121
<font color="#FFFFFF">Modern deep learning is full of feedback loops.</font>

23
00:01:30,201 --> 00:01:35,094
<font color="#FFFFFF">A language model's next token depends on the tokens
it already generated.</font>

24
00:01:35,174 --> 00:01:39,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">A robot's next observation depends on the action it
just took.</font>

25
00:01:39,397 --> 00:01:44,834
<font color="#FFFFFF">But when we train these systems, we almost always
optimize a surrogate objective,</font>

26
00:01:44,914 --> 00:01:49,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">like next-token prediction, that pretends the
feedback loop is not there.</font>

27
00:01:49,886 --> 00:01:52,599
<font color="#FFFFFF">That surrogate tends to improve smoothly,</font>

28
00:01:52,679 --> 00:01:56,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">which lulls us into thinking training is well behaved.</font>

29
00:01:56,357 --> 00:01:59,479
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors argue this is a dangerous illusion,</font>

30
00:01:59,559 --> 00:02:04,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that the real culprit behind the instability is
not the data or the model,</font>

31
00:02:04,872 --> 00:02:07,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the noise in the optimizer itself.</font>

32
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:11,461
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

33
00:02:11,541 --> 00:02:15,243
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

34
00:02:15,323 --> 00:02:19,024
<font color="#FFFFFF">through an extensive study of two hundred and
seventy-eight distinct interventions,</font>

35
00:02:19,104 --> 00:02:22,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">it diagnoses these reward oscillations and names
the mechanism gradient variance amplification,</font>

36
00:02:22,886 --> 00:02:26,587
<font color="#FFFFFF">or G V A,</font>

37
00:02:26,667 --> 00:02:30,369
<font color="#FFFFFF">the propagation of minibatch stochastic gradient
noise through the closed-loop dynamics.</font>

38
00:02:30,449 --> 00:02:34,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it shows the problem is algorithmic rather
than statistical,</font>

39
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:37,931
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that an exponential moving average of the
weights fixes it,</font>

40
00:02:38,011 --> 00:02:41,713
<font color="#FFFFFF">even though most standard mitigations fail.</font>

41
00:02:41,793 --> 00:02:45,494
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

42
00:02:45,574 --> 00:02:49,276
<font color="#FFFFFF">it demonstrates the exact same phenomenon and the
same cure in autoregressive language models,</font>

43
00:02:49,356 --> 00:02:53,057
<font color="#FFFFFF">and complements everything with a convex theory vignette.</font>

44
00:02:53,137 --> 00:02:56,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">The upshot is a call to treat iterate averaging as
an essential design choice,</font>

45
00:02:56,919 --> 00:03:00,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">a stabilizer that belongs alongside your optimizer
and your scheduler.</font>

46
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,875
<font color="#FFFFFF">The approach is first to carefully separate the two
possible causes of the oscillations.</font>

47
00:03:06,955 --> 00:03:12,086
<font color="#FFFFFF">Is it a statistical problem, meaning not enough
data, or a computational one,</font>

48
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:15,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">meaning something about the optimization itself?</font>

49
00:03:15,414 --> 00:03:20,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">By ablating away the statistical difficulty and
still seeing the oscillations,</font>

50
00:03:20,692 --> 00:03:24,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors pin the blame on gradient variance
amplification.</font>

51
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:26,973
<font color="#FFFFFF">Then they test the natural fixes.</font>

52
00:03:27,053 --> 00:03:30,086
<font color="#FFFFFF">Aggressively decaying the learning rate helps,</font>

53
00:03:30,166 --> 00:03:35,567
<font color="#FFFFFF">and dramatically increasing the batch size helps,
but both cost a lot of compute.</font>

54
00:03:35,647 --> 00:03:38,139
<font color="#FFFFFF">The star of the paper is much cheaper.</font>

55
00:03:38,219 --> 00:03:42,808
<font color="#FFFFFF">You simply keep a running exponential moving
average of your weights,</font>

56
00:03:42,888 --> 00:03:44,364
<font color="#FFFFFF">updating it every step,</font>

57
00:03:44,444 --> 00:03:50,184
<font color="#FFFFFF">defined by theta-tilde at time t plus one equals
one minus gamma times the old average</font>

58
00:03:50,264 --> 00:03:52,417
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus gamma times the new iterate.</font>

59
00:03:52,497 --> 00:03:57,154
<font color="#FFFFFF">A supporting theorem shows that in a simple
deterministic environment,</font>

60
00:03:57,234 --> 00:04:03,177
<font color="#FFFFFF">a tiny perturbation that barely changes the
imitation loss can blow up the rollout reward</font>

61
00:04:03,257 --> 00:04:05,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">gap exponentially in the horizon.</font>

62
00:04:05,790 --> 00:04:09,941
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments span two very different domains.</font>

63
00:04:10,021 --> 00:04:14,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">For continuous control, the authors use classic
MuJoCo locomotion tasks,</font>

64
00:04:14,252 --> 00:04:18,403
<font color="#FFFFFF">with Walker2d as the primary testbed and Hopper,
HalfCheetah, Ant,</font>

65
00:04:18,483 --> 00:04:22,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Humanoid for breadth.</font>

66
00:04:22,714 --> 00:04:26,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">They clone from strong expert policies over a long
horizon of a thousand steps,</font>

67
00:04:26,945 --> 00:04:31,095
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they deliberately set the dataset size so that
overfitting is not the issue.</font>

68
00:04:31,175 --> 00:04:35,326
<font color="#FFFFFF">For language,</font>

69
00:04:35,406 --> 00:04:39,557
<font color="#FFFFFF">they train two hundred and seventy million
parameter Transformers on TinyStories,</font>

70
00:04:39,637 --> 00:04:43,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">a small synthetic dataset of simple children's
stories that keeps the setup tractable</font>

71
00:04:43,868 --> 00:04:48,019
<font color="#FFFFFF">while still exhibiting the phenomenon.</font>

72
00:04:48,099 --> 00:04:52,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reward is averaged over twenty initial conditions
so the oscillations are not just measurement noise.</font>

73
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:54,757
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is striking.</font>

74
00:04:54,837 --> 00:05:01,379
<font color="#FFFFFF">Plotting rollout reward against training step
reveals dramatic swings between consecutive iterates,</font>

75
00:05:01,459 --> 00:05:05,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">and zooming in shows a jagged, almost fractal
reward landscape,</font>

76
00:05:05,673 --> 00:05:09,406
<font color="#FFFFFF">all while the behavior cloning loss sits flat and smooth.</font>

77
00:05:09,486 --> 00:05:13,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">That jaggedness is completely invisible in the
one-step objective.</font>

78
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:16,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">Now apply the exponential moving average,</font>

79
00:05:16,643 --> 00:05:22,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">and those oscillations are dramatically damped,
across architectures, dataset sizes,</font>

80
00:05:22,261 --> 00:05:27,465
<font color="#FFFFFF">and multiple tasks, with no change to the learning
rate schedule or batch size.</font>

81
00:05:27,545 --> 00:05:31,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the same butterfly effect shows up in language
generation,</font>

82
00:05:31,692 --> 00:05:38,703
<font color="#FFFFFF">where two nearly identical training checkpoints
produce stories that diverge into totally different plots.</font>

83
00:05:38,783 --> 00:05:44,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here too the moving average tames the instability
and even yields the lowest-perplexity model.</font>

84
00:05:45,370 --> 00:05:48,984
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablations are what make the diagnosis convincing.</font>

85
00:05:49,064 --> 00:05:53,165
<font color="#FFFFFF">The oscillations refuse to go away as you vary
dataset size,</font>

86
00:05:53,245 --> 00:05:59,089
<font color="#FFFFFF">swap a multilayer perceptron for a Transformer,
scale the model, or add regularizers.</font>

87
00:05:59,169 --> 00:06:02,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">Only genuine variance reduction consistently calms them,</font>

88
00:06:03,072 --> 00:06:07,941
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exactly what you would predict if gradient
noise is the cause.</font>

89
00:06:08,021 --> 00:06:13,795
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the fix side, the moving average works best when
you update it every single step,</font>

90
00:06:13,875 --> 00:06:19,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">use an initial burn-in period, and anneal the
averaging rate with a polynomial decay.</font>

91
00:06:19,799 --> 00:06:23,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">Skip the burn-in, or update the average only occasionally,</font>

92
00:06:23,841 --> 00:06:26,201
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its stabilizing power degrades.</font>

93
00:06:26,281 --> 00:06:29,546
<font color="#FFFFFF">Learning rate decay and giant batches also work,</font>

94
00:06:29,626 --> 00:06:33,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they cost far more compute for the same benefit.</font>

95
00:06:33,550 --> 00:06:35,615
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers anchor the study.</font>

96
00:06:35,695 --> 00:06:43,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">The empirical investigation covers two hundred and
seventy-eight distinct interventions in continuous control.</font>

97
00:06:43,305 --> 00:06:46,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">The rollouts run over a horizon of one thousand steps,</font>

98
00:06:47,041 --> 00:06:53,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the theory shows the error amplification
constant grows exponentially in that horizon.</font>

99
00:06:53,268 --> 00:06:54,641
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the language side,</font>

100
00:06:54,721 --> 00:07:01,075
<font color="#FFFFFF">the models are two hundred and seventy million
parameter Transformers trained on TinyStories.</font>

101
00:07:01,155 --> 00:07:09,239
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key contrast in the theory is that a
perturbation changes the behavior cloning loss by only order H delta squared,</font>

102
00:07:09,319 --> 00:07:14,635
<font color="#FFFFFF">but can change the rollout reward by order H times
e to the H delta minus one,</font>

103
00:07:14,715 --> 00:07:15,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">an exponential gap.</font>

104
00:07:16,330 --> 00:07:18,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple and actionable.</font>

105
00:07:18,883 --> 00:07:24,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">Stochastic gradient noise is completely harmless
for the one-step training loss you watch,</font>

106
00:07:24,930 --> 00:07:28,075
<font color="#FFFFFF">but once your model sits inside a feedback loop,</font>

107
00:07:28,155 --> 00:07:31,032
<font color="#FFFFFF">that same noise gets amplified into chaotic,</font>

108
00:07:31,112 --> 00:07:33,786
<font color="#FFFFFF">catastrophic failures over long horizons.</font>

109
00:07:33,866 --> 00:07:36,944
<font color="#FFFFFF">They call this gradient variance amplification.</font>

110
00:07:37,024 --> 00:07:39,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">The good news is that the fix is almost free.</font>

111
00:07:40,048 --> 00:07:44,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">Keep an exponential moving average of your weights,
and you get a cheap,</font>

112
00:07:44,886 --> 00:07:46,754
<font color="#FFFFFF">broadly effective stabilizer.</font>

113
00:07:46,834 --> 00:07:51,054
<font color="#FFFFFF">Whether you are training a robot controller or a
language model,</font>

114
00:07:51,134 --> 00:07:55,421
<font color="#FFFFFF">iterate averaging deserves to be a standard part of
the pipeline,</font>

115
00:07:55,501 --> 00:07:59,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">right next to your optimizer and your learning rate
schedule.</font>
